
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. In none of the three gospels does Jesus say what the cloth, the garment, the wine or the bottles are, and none of the three records a request for an explanation or an answer to one. Matthew records the comparisons and moves to the ruler (9:18). Mark records them and moves to the corn fields (2:23). Luke records them with one more saying (5:39) and moves to another Sabbath (6:1). This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What stands beside the comparisons. Just before the comparisons Jesus speaks of the children of the bridechamber and of the bridegroom: “Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast” (Matthew 9:15). Those words are His answer to the question about fasting. They are not given as an explanation of the cloth or the wine. Matthew has no joining word between them and the cloth, and Mark and Luke have “also.” Jesus does not say that the cloth, the garment, the wine or the bottles stand for the bridegroom, the fasting or anything else. In these verses Jesus does not state who the bridegroom is.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not identify. Each of the following is left without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the man who puts the piece on the garment. Jesus does not identify the new cloth or the old garment. Jesus does not identify the rent. Jesus does not identify the new wine or the old wine. Jesus does not identify the old bottles or the new bottles. Jesus does not identify the bursting, the spilling or the perishing. Jesus does not identify the man who has drunk old wine, and He does not say whether his saying is right.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the stories', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The man or men who put in the piece or the wine', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 9:16–17; Mark 2:21–22; Luke 5:36–38'],
    ['The new cloth, the new piece, the old garment', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 9:16; Mark 2:21; Luke 5:36'],
    ['The rent', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 9:16; Mark 2:21; Luke 5:36'],
    ['The new wine and the old wine', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 9:17; Mark 2:22; Luke 5:37–39'],
    ['The old bottles and the new bottles', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 9:17; Mark 2:22; Luke 5:37–38'],
    ['The man who has drunk old wine', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Luke 5:39'],
], [0.34, 0.30, 0.36]))
A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the new cloth is a certain message, that the old garment is a certain law, that the new wine is a certain gift or that the bottles are a certain people is supplying what the Lord did not. The saying about the bridegroom (Matthew 9:15) is Jesus’ answer to a question about fasting, and it is not carried into the cloth or the wine as an explanation.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text does say. The text says that no man puts a piece of new cloth on an old garment, because the piece takes from the garment and the rent is made worse. It says that men do not put new wine into old bottles, because the bottles break and the wine runs out, but that new wine is put into new bottles and both are preserved. Luke records one more saying, that a man who has drunk old wine does not straightway desire new, because he says, “The old is better.” These statements are made by the stories themselves and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for these comparisons, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparisons themselves. It assigns no identity to any part of them.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparisons. Jesus says that no man puts a piece of new cloth on an old garment, because the piece takes from the garment and the tear is made worse. He says that men do not put new wine into old bottles, because the bottles break and the wine is lost, and that new wine is put into new bottles so that both are preserved. The meaning is in what the two stories show about putting new things into old things and about what is lost or kept.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparisons communicate.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**New and old are not joined without loss.** The new piece does not mend the old garment. It “taketh from the garment,” and “the rent is made worse” (Matthew 9:16). Luke says the piece “agreeth not with the old” (5:36).',
    '**Putting new into old can destroy both.** New wine put into old bottles breaks the bottles and loses the wine (Matthew 9:17). Mark says the bottles are burst and marred and the wine is spilled (2:22).',
    '**What fits is kept.** New wine put into new bottles has the result Matthew and Luke state: “both are preserved” (Matthew 9:17; Luke 5:38). Mark states that new wine “must be put into new bottles” (2:22).',
    '**The man who prefers the old speaks from habit.** Luke records the saying of the man who has drunk old wine: “The old is better” (5:39). The verse gives it as his saying and does not say whether it is right.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were those who had put the question about fasting and had seen the feast with publicans and sinners. They were meant to understand that Jesus was setting before them comparisons about what is suitable to put with what, and about what is lost when the unsuitable is joined. The text records no answer from the hearers, and it does not say how they took the comparisons.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the loss and the preserving. Two ways of acting are set side by side in each story: one that makes the harm worse or destroys the vessel and the wine, and one that keeps both. The hearer is asked to see that the way of acting has a result, and that the result can be a loss that cannot be mended.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. A new piece does not mend an old garment. New wine does not belong in old bottles. What is put with what makes the difference between loss and keeping. A man who is used to the old does not straightway want the new, and Luke records his reason without ruling on it. These statements arise from the comparisons, and they do not say that the cloth, the garment, the wine or the bottles represent anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say that the old garment is a law or a covenant, that the new wine is a message or a spirit, or that the bottles are a people or the Body. It does not say that the comparisons answer the question about fasting. It does not turn following, feasting or putting in into steps by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another passage into these comparisons.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The comparisons of the cloth and the wine are recorded in three gospels in one sequence. Other passages in the same gospels speak of eating, drinking, fasting and publicans and sinners, and they are kept apart from these comparisons here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 11:16–19', '“We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced”; “Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners”', 'The Barren Fig Tree and the Generation’s Response reads this with Luke 7:31–35. It is a comparison of this generation to children, spoken after John’s question. It does not contain a cloth, a garment or a bottle.'],
    ['Luke 15:1–2', '“This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them”', 'The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin reads this setting. The murmuring is again about eating with sinners, but the parables told there are a sheep and a coin.'],
    ['Matt 7:24–27', '“I will liken him unto a wise man, which built his house upon a rock”', 'The Wise and Foolish Builders is a comparison of a man who hears and does with a man who hears and does not. It stands in the Sermon on the Mount and not at Matthew 9.'],
    ['Mark 2:23–28', '“the sabbath was made for man”', 'The Sabbath scene that follows the comparisons in Mark. It is a separate scene and is not part of the cloth or the wine.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The cloth and the wine come after the call of the tax collector, the feast and the question about fasting. The generation passage of Matthew 11 and Luke 7 comes after John’s question from prison. The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin are told in a setting where publicans and sinners draw near to hear Jesus (Luke 15:1). A shared word such as “eat” or “publicans and sinners” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The cloth and the wine have a man or men, a garment, bottles and wine. The Lost Sheep has a shepherd and a sheep. The Wise and Foolish Builders have two men and two houses. No character is carried from one passage into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The cloth moves from a patch to a worse tear. The wine moves from new wine in old bottles to broken bottles and spilled wine, and then to new wine in new bottles. The generation passage moves from children calling in the market to a statement about John and the Son of man. None of those movements is read into another.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the bridegroom of Matthew 9:15 is not read into the cloth or the wine as their explanation. Second, the eating and drinking of Matthew 11:19 and Luke 7:34 are not read into the wine of Matthew 9:17. Third, “the old is better” in Luke 5:39 is not carried into the other two accounts, which do not contain it.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks in the ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17), went about “preaching the gospel of the kingdom” (9:35), and sent the Twelve “to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The comparisons of the cloth and the wine belong to that ministry. They are spoken to people who had put a question about fasting and who had seen the feast at the tax collector’s house.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The comparisons were spoken before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. These passages contain no word about the Mystery, and they are not read as if the Body were being described in them. Nothing in the cloth or the wine tells a reader what the Body is.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The cloth, the garment, the wine and the bottles are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The comparisons are not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The calling of Matthew, his following and the feast are actions in the story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in these comparisons: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the stories show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of fitness.** The new piece does not suit the old garment, and the new wine does not suit the old bottles. A reader learns to ask whether a thing is suited to what it is joined to before joining it.',
    '**A lesson of foresight.** In each story the result is stated before the act is done. A reader learns to count what a step will cost before taking it.',
    '**A lesson of care for what is valuable.** The wine is lost when the bottle breaks, and both are kept when the bottle is fit. A reader learns to guard what is worth keeping.',
    '**A lesson of honesty about habit.** The man who has drunk old wine says, “The old is better” (Luke 5:39). A reader learns to notice when a preference rests on familiarity, without the text ruling that old or new is better.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom ministry is not turned into the Body’s hope, and the garment and the wine are not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in these comparisons.'))
